1
00:00:02,460 --> 00:00:07,381
Every day in Hong Kong,
about four people die of liver cancer.

2
00:00:07,970 --> 00:00:12,524
That's 1,412 people in 2022 alone.

3
00:00:12,895 --> 00:00:18,510
Here, it's one of the three deadliest
cancers, alongside lung and colorectal.

4
00:00:18,801 --> 00:00:20,152
And it's not just us.

5
00:00:20,342 --> 00:00:25,357
Worldwide, it's the sixth most common
cancer, and the third deadliest.

6
00:00:25,623 --> 00:00:28,532
Here's the thing.
We already have medicines.

7
00:00:28,572 --> 00:00:30,105
Just not good enough ones.

8
00:00:30,670 --> 00:00:35,544
The standard first treatment for advanced
liver cancer has already changed once,

9
00:00:35,934 --> 00:00:41,609
from Nexavar to a combo,
atezolizumab plus bevacizumab.

10
00:00:41,900 --> 00:00:47,074
But here, only about three in ten
patients are alive five years on.

11
00:00:47,365 --> 00:00:51,718
The study we follow calls
it a huge unmet need.

12
00:00:51,986 --> 00:00:55,021
So... why not just make a better one?

13
00:00:55,606 --> 00:00:57,763
Because that's way harder than it sounds.

14
00:00:57,973 --> 00:01:00,968
Like, ten-to-seventeen-years hard.

15
00:01:01,537 --> 00:01:06,593
Start the search the year we were born,
and it might only be ready now.

16
00:01:06,884 --> 00:01:10,738
It can cost almost 2.8 billion US dollars.

17
00:01:11,008 --> 00:01:16,965
And of drugs tested in people,
only about one in ten reaches the market.

18
00:01:17,037 --> 00:01:20,308
To see why,
let's follow one drug's journey.

19
00:01:20,719 --> 00:01:24,991
Every medicine walks the same road.
Find a target.

20
00:01:25,141 --> 00:01:30,494
Find a hit, a first molecule
that works on it. Improve it.

21
00:01:30,644 --> 00:01:33,595
Test it in cells, then animals.

22
00:01:33,985 --> 00:01:39,537
Then human trials: phase one, two, three.
Then approval.

23
00:01:39,687 --> 00:01:41,077
Any step can fail.

24
00:01:41,337 --> 00:01:43,971
Now here's where it gets interesting.

25
00:01:44,322 --> 00:01:45,774
In 2024,

26
00:01:46,045 --> 00:01:51,113
part of the Nobel Prize in Chemistry
went to Demis Hassabis and John Jumper,

27
00:01:51,153 --> 00:01:55,089
for AlphaFold,
an AI that predicts the shape of proteins.

28
00:01:55,303 --> 00:01:58,278
So can AI walk the first steps faster?

29
00:01:58,849 --> 00:02:02,875
Let's follow one real case,
where AI tools took on liver cancer.

30
00:02:02,915 --> 00:02:06,751
And part of it starts right here,
in Hong Kong.

31
00:02:18,837 --> 00:02:20,088
Hong Kong first.

32
00:02:20,178 --> 00:02:25,662
In 2011, a team at the Chinese University
of Hong Kong studied liver tumours

33
00:02:25,702 --> 00:02:28,794
from patients at the
Prince of Wales Hospital.

34
00:02:29,344 --> 00:02:35,078
One protein kept showing up raised:
CCRK, also called CDK20.

35
00:02:35,628 --> 00:02:37,560
Fast forward to 2023:

36
00:02:37,670 --> 00:02:41,242
an AI drug-discovery study
goes after the same protein,

37
00:02:41,292 --> 00:02:46,085
with co-authors at Insilico Medicine's
Hong Kong office in Science Park.

38
00:02:46,751 --> 00:02:48,824
So our research question is:

39
00:02:49,015 --> 00:02:53,361
how can computer-aided
methods help scientists find

40
00:02:53,401 --> 00:02:56,816
and assess potential
cancer drug candidates?

41
00:02:57,470 --> 00:03:03,327
And we'll answer it with one case study:
CDK20 in liver cancer.

42
00:03:03,581 --> 00:03:06,497
We set ourselves two objectives.

43
00:03:07,170 --> 00:03:11,717
One: to identify the major
computer-aided methods used

44
00:03:11,757 --> 00:03:13,649
in cancer drug discovery.

45
00:03:13,840 --> 00:03:17,596
Two: to evaluate how these methods help

46
00:03:17,646 --> 00:03:21,041
scientists assess
potential drug candidates.

47
00:03:21,103 --> 00:03:24,974
So how did we check all this?
We read. A lot.

48
00:03:25,304 --> 00:03:28,855
No lab work of our own:
this is a literature review.

49
00:03:29,205 --> 00:03:33,936
At the centre, one original study by
Ren and colleagues in Chemical Science,

50
00:03:33,976 --> 00:03:37,717
2023, with its supplementary data.

51
00:03:38,067 --> 00:03:41,398
Around it, the earlier Hong Kong study,

52
00:03:41,728 --> 00:03:45,979
papers behind two AI tools, a 2023 review,

53
00:03:46,389 --> 00:03:51,480
a textbook chapter,
and a 2026 expert Perspective,

54
00:03:51,830 --> 00:03:54,761
which we read only as an abstract.

55
00:03:54,870 --> 00:03:57,501
For every source, we asked:

56
00:03:57,851 --> 00:04:03,184
what kind is it, how much did we read,
and how strong is the evidence?

57
00:04:03,915 --> 00:04:07,867
Who did the work?
Were there controls and repeats?

58
00:04:08,638 --> 00:04:10,168
One thing worth knowing:

59
00:04:10,379 --> 00:04:15,712
most authors of the main study
work for Insilico Medicine,

60
00:04:16,162 --> 00:04:18,794
which built its AI tools.

61
00:04:19,124 --> 00:04:20,815
They say so openly.

62
00:04:20,945 --> 00:04:24,738
Useful evidence, not an independent check.

63
00:04:24,837 --> 00:04:26,708
So here's where we're going.

64
00:04:27,138 --> 00:04:28,669
First, the lock.

65
00:04:29,459 --> 00:04:32,271
Then how AI helped cut a key for it.

66
00:04:32,901 --> 00:04:34,712
Then what happened in the lab.

67
00:04:35,143 --> 00:04:40,296
And finally, the big one:
how much did the computers really help?

68
00:04:40,666 --> 00:04:46,262
Okay.
To get this, picture a lock and a key.

69
00:04:46,570 --> 00:04:50,585
Inside your cells,
proteins do a lot of the work.

70
00:04:50,916 --> 00:04:55,352
Most drugs grab one protein
tightly and change what it does,

71
00:04:55,843 --> 00:04:59,438
slotting into a pocket
like a key into a lock.

72
00:04:59,503 --> 00:05:04,697
Scientists call the lock the target,
and the key a candidate molecule.

73
00:05:04,847 --> 00:05:09,541
A good key fits its own lock tightly,
and opens no one else's.

74
00:05:09,912 --> 00:05:11,643
That's called selectivity.

75
00:05:11,703 --> 00:05:13,715
Our lock is CDK20.

76
00:05:13,965 --> 00:05:17,499
It's an enzyme,
a protein with a chemical job.

77
00:05:17,689 --> 00:05:23,185
Its keyhole is a pocket where it normally
grabs a small molecule called ATP.

78
00:05:23,736 --> 00:05:28,791
Plug that pocket with the right key,
and the enzyme can't do its job.

79
00:05:28,870 --> 00:05:31,422
Why this lock?
Remember the Hong Kong team.

80
00:05:31,493 --> 00:05:34,145
In 36 of 52 liver tumours,

81
00:05:34,216 --> 00:05:38,210
CDK20 was higher than in
nearby non-tumour tissue.

82
00:05:38,380 --> 00:05:42,534
High levels went with later-stage
tumours and shorter survival.

83
00:05:42,585 --> 00:05:47,580
And turning CDK20 down in liver
cancer cells slowed their growth.

84
00:05:48,239 --> 00:05:51,814
So: a strong suspect.

85
00:05:52,947 --> 00:05:58,826
Not the only cause of liver cancer,
but a lock worth trying.

86
00:05:59,403 --> 00:06:05,036
In 2023, an AI platform called
PandaOmics backed that hunch.

87
00:06:05,406 --> 00:06:07,657
It combed ten liver cancer data sets,

88
00:06:08,067 --> 00:06:11,578
over a thousand tumour samples
and hundreds of healthy ones,

89
00:06:11,688 --> 00:06:13,259
plus research papers.

90
00:06:13,869 --> 00:06:19,301
People checked its top 20 by hand,
and CDK20 came out on top,

91
00:06:19,692 --> 00:06:22,123
with no approved drug against it yet.

92
00:06:22,203 --> 00:06:24,305
But here's the problem.
To design a key, you need to see the lock.

93
00:06:24,345 --> 00:06:25,797
And no one had measured CDK20's shape.

94
00:06:26,147 --> 00:06:28,659
That takes a lot of hard lab work,

95
00:06:29,070 --> 00:06:33,263
and only a small fraction of
proteins have ever been measured.

96
00:06:33,613 --> 00:06:39,569
Without a 3D shape, even computer
predictions of binding get expensive,

97
00:06:39,839 --> 00:06:41,430
slow and difficult.

98
00:06:42,084 --> 00:06:44,537
So you've got a lock nobody can see.

99
00:06:45,269 --> 00:06:47,062
How do you cut a key in the dark?

100
00:07:12,300 --> 00:07:17,073
So the team did something that
once sounded like science fiction.

101
00:07:17,423 --> 00:07:20,175
They asked an AI to guess the shape.

102
00:07:20,725 --> 00:07:26,059
Imagine a protein as a chain of building
blocks that folds into a 3D shape.

103
00:07:26,549 --> 00:07:28,981
The order of the blocks is the sequence.

104
00:07:29,571 --> 00:07:33,964
AlphaFold learned from known
structures how sequences fold.

105
00:07:34,154 --> 00:07:37,186
Give it one, and it predicts the shape,

106
00:07:37,397 --> 00:07:40,560
plus how sure it is about each part.

107
00:07:40,600 --> 00:07:44,532
Before,
you'd need a lab-measured structure. Now?

108
00:07:44,842 --> 00:07:50,394
The team just downloaded a prediction,
for free, from a public database.

109
00:07:51,145 --> 00:07:56,957
According to the paper, this was the
first time AlphaFold had been used

110
00:07:57,287 --> 00:07:59,278
to find a hit like this.

111
00:07:59,337 --> 00:08:00,467
But look closer.

112
00:08:01,138 --> 00:08:06,650
One end of the protein, a floppy tail,
had really low confidence.

113
00:08:07,361 --> 00:08:10,612
And in the model,
it lay across the keyhole,

114
00:08:11,062 --> 00:08:14,774
with one building block
sitting inside the ATP pocket.

115
00:08:15,124 --> 00:08:19,255
Trust it blindly,
and you'd cut keys for a blocked door.

116
00:08:19,550 --> 00:08:21,102
So what did they do?

117
00:08:21,440 --> 00:08:24,053
They cut the tail off.
The AI made the guess.

118
00:08:24,444 --> 00:08:26,076
A human caught the problem.

119
00:08:27,070 --> 00:08:29,922
And this next part honestly blew my mind.

120
00:08:30,273 --> 00:08:33,346
Think about how chemists
have always found a key.

121
00:08:33,836 --> 00:08:39,362
Imagine a molecule. Make it.
Test it. It fails. Tweak it.

122
00:08:39,402 --> 00:08:44,697
Try again. And every single round
in a real lab costs time and money.

123
00:08:44,770 --> 00:08:48,901
Generative AI takes that loop
and moves it inside a computer.

124
00:08:49,291 --> 00:08:52,342
The platform here, Chemistry42,

125
00:08:52,713 --> 00:08:57,604
runs dozens of AI models side by side,
built on known chemistry.

126
00:08:58,135 --> 00:09:01,346
They invent new molecules
for this exact keyhole,

127
00:09:01,516 --> 00:09:04,927
score them, learn from the scores,
and go again.

128
00:09:05,377 --> 00:09:09,069
Its makers say it turns out
new structures within hours.

129
00:09:09,719 --> 00:09:11,911
AI didn't replace the scientists.

130
00:09:12,182 --> 00:09:17,396
It ran their trial and error
inside a computer, at light speed.

131
00:09:18,003 --> 00:09:21,514
The team gave it the
pocket's shape and one rule:

132
00:09:21,944 --> 00:09:24,834
every key must grip a
spot called the hinge.

133
00:09:25,184 --> 00:09:31,184
Its filters are built to toss designs
with reactive or possibly toxic groups,

134
00:09:31,685 --> 00:09:35,516
and to score how drug-like
and easy to make each one is.

135
00:09:50,637 --> 00:09:55,210
It came back with 8,918 brand-new designs.

136
00:09:55,461 --> 00:09:56,972
Way too many to make.

137
00:09:57,062 --> 00:09:59,854
So each key gets tried in a virtual lock.

138
00:10:00,205 --> 00:10:04,068
That's docking: the computer
predicts how a molecule might sit

139
00:10:04,108 --> 00:10:06,479
in the pocket, and scores the fit.

140
00:10:07,090 --> 00:10:11,303
Similar designs get grouped,
54 make the shortlist,

141
00:10:11,434 --> 00:10:14,025
and chemists pick just seven to make.

142
00:10:14,257 --> 00:10:17,568
Wait. So the computer picked the winners?

143
00:10:17,608 --> 00:10:22,583
Not quite.
A docking score is a clue, not proof.

144
00:10:22,854 --> 00:10:24,245
Only the lab can tell.

145
00:10:24,570 --> 00:10:26,381
So, the moment of truth.

146
00:10:26,712 --> 00:10:30,105
Seven real molecules, made in a real lab.

147
00:10:30,495 --> 00:10:33,608
Do any actually grab CDK20?

148
00:10:34,018 --> 00:10:36,130
Grip is measured as Kd:

149
00:10:36,320 --> 00:10:40,164
roughly, how much molecule it takes

150
00:10:40,204 --> 00:10:42,576
to grab half the protein.

151
00:10:42,646 --> 00:10:44,197
Lower means tighter.

152
00:10:44,654 --> 00:10:46,529
Six of them barely held on.

153
00:10:46,680 --> 00:10:50,059
But one stood out. Its name? 001.

154
00:10:51,203 --> 00:10:56,277
001's Kd: 9.2 micromolar. The other six?

155
00:10:56,568 --> 00:10:59,160
All above 30. Much weaker.

156
00:10:59,550 --> 00:11:01,221
And here's the wild part.

157
00:11:01,752 --> 00:11:04,164
The clock read just 30 days,

158
00:11:04,214 --> 00:11:08,067
from picking the target to
this first confirmed hit.

159
00:11:08,137 --> 00:11:10,428
But then came a second test.

160
00:11:10,638 --> 00:11:15,290
Because grabbing a lock
isn't the same as jamming it.

161
00:11:15,780 --> 00:11:20,032
Does 001 actually stop CDK20 from working?

162
00:11:20,542 --> 00:11:23,473
That's measured as IC50:

163
00:11:23,784 --> 00:11:27,875
how much you need to cut the
enzyme's activity in half.

164
00:11:28,086 --> 00:11:29,486
For 001?

165
00:11:29,526 --> 00:11:31,917
Above 6,000 nanomolar. Weak.

166
00:11:32,179 --> 00:11:34,203
The key went into the lock.

167
00:11:34,243 --> 00:11:36,036
But it barely turned.

168
00:11:36,173 --> 00:11:39,750
So was that it? Back to square one?

169
00:11:40,337 --> 00:11:45,189
Not at all.
This is where the loop kicks in again,

170
00:11:45,459 --> 00:11:48,170
now with real lab data inside.

171
00:11:48,280 --> 00:11:49,991
The results went back in,

172
00:11:50,421 --> 00:11:54,953
with a predicted pose of
how 001 sat in the pocket.

173
00:11:55,263 --> 00:11:56,644
Sixteen new designs.

174
00:11:56,684 --> 00:11:58,274
Six made and tested.

175
00:11:58,505 --> 00:12:01,576
All within 30 days of that first hit.

176
00:12:01,670 --> 00:12:07,315
And one of them, 048,
grabbed about fifteen times tighter.

177
00:12:07,355 --> 00:12:09,847
Then the enzyme test.

178
00:12:10,137 --> 00:12:15,432
Remember,
001 needed more than 6,000 nanomolar.

179
00:12:15,723 --> 00:12:19,976
048 needed just 33.4.

180
00:12:20,426 --> 00:12:23,046
This time, the key turned.

181
00:12:31,503 --> 00:12:34,475
Then, the big step: living cells.

182
00:12:35,005 --> 00:12:38,368
After three days with 048,

183
00:12:38,408 --> 00:12:42,700
Huh7 liver cancer cells,
packed with CDK20,

184
00:12:43,131 --> 00:12:46,383
lost half their living-cell signal

185
00:12:46,553 --> 00:12:50,305
at about 210 nanomolar.

186
00:12:50,636 --> 00:12:54,938
Comparison cells, HEK293,

187
00:12:54,978 --> 00:12:58,471
needed about 1,700.

188
00:12:58,621 --> 00:13:00,563
Roughly eight times more.

189
00:13:00,603 --> 00:13:02,216
So, the big question.

190
00:13:02,508 --> 00:13:05,344
How much did the computers actually help?

191
00:13:05,616 --> 00:13:07,791
Let's line up before and now.

192
00:13:07,870 --> 00:13:13,015
Seeing the lock. Before: hard lab
experiments, and without a 3D shape,

193
00:13:13,055 --> 00:13:15,887
design was expensive, slow and difficult.

194
00:13:16,038 --> 00:13:20,592
Now: a free prediction,
downloaded from a public database.

195
00:13:20,670 --> 00:13:24,262
Finding a hit. Before:
screening real compounds by the millions,

196
00:13:24,453 --> 00:13:29,165
an approach a 2023 review says
has hit a costly bottleneck.

197
00:13:29,336 --> 00:13:33,138
Now: 8,918 designs inside a computer,

198
00:13:33,178 --> 00:13:35,509
and just 13 molecules actually made.

199
00:13:35,989 --> 00:13:41,454
Millions of compounds screened before.
Here? Thirteen made.

200
00:13:41,590 --> 00:13:44,472
Thirteen. That's honestly wild.

201
00:13:45,070 --> 00:13:48,855
Time.
Before: a ten-to-seventeen-year road,

202
00:13:48,895 --> 00:13:51,920
with finding a hit just
the opening stretch.

203
00:13:51,960 --> 00:13:56,556
Now: 30 days to the first hit,
and a better key within another 30.

204
00:13:56,637 --> 00:14:01,613
Improving the key. Before: every
change meant another costly lab round.

205
00:14:01,724 --> 00:14:06,420
Now: a predicted picture of the
first key guided sixteen designs,

206
00:14:06,610 --> 00:14:09,664
and the grip got about
fifteen times tighter.

207
00:14:09,737 --> 00:14:13,080
To be fair,
these pairs come from different studies:

208
00:14:13,120 --> 00:14:17,433
they compare scale and speed,
not one race run side by side.

209
00:14:17,704 --> 00:14:22,318
But the paper itself calls the
work cost- and time-efficient.

210
00:14:22,403 --> 00:14:25,009
further down the road.
Fewer failed molecules, lower costs,

211
00:14:25,160 --> 00:14:28,034
fewer animal tests, less chemical waste.

212
00:14:28,405 --> 00:14:32,501
Computing costs electricity too.
And the real prize?

213
00:14:33,032 --> 00:14:34,397
New treatments for patients.

214
00:14:34,437 --> 00:14:37,510
So where's 048 on the road?

215
00:14:37,660 --> 00:14:39,271
Target: found.

216
00:14:39,702 --> 00:14:41,093
Hit: found.

217
00:14:41,243 --> 00:14:43,315
Improving it: started.

218
00:14:43,866 --> 00:14:46,359
Cells: first tests, done.

219
00:14:46,509 --> 00:14:49,602
Animals and clinical trials: not yet.

220
00:14:49,730 --> 00:14:52,404
And here's what the end of
that road can look like.

221
00:14:52,554 --> 00:14:56,145
Some AI-assisted cancer drugs
have walked much further.

222
00:14:56,295 --> 00:15:01,466
In September 2026,
one of them, lirafugratinib,

223
00:15:01,616 --> 00:15:05,067
was approved in the US
for a bile duct cancer.

224
00:15:05,217 --> 00:15:08,569
Others failed along the way. And 048?

225
00:15:08,719 --> 00:15:11,790
Its makers chose not to take it to trials,

226
00:15:11,940 --> 00:15:14,552
and shared it with other
researchers instead.

227
00:15:15,084 --> 00:15:17,561
048 is at the very start of the road.

228
00:15:17,853 --> 00:15:21,533
But for this case,
that start took weeks, not years.

229
00:15:22,853 --> 00:15:24,708
So, what should happen next?

230
00:15:25,140 --> 00:15:27,175
We'd suggest three things.

231
00:15:27,870 --> 00:15:30,821
One: walk 048 further.

232
00:15:31,091 --> 00:15:33,742
It's public now, so anyone could try.

233
00:15:34,073 --> 00:15:37,885
Test it in more liver cancer
cells and normal liver cells,

234
00:15:38,135 --> 00:15:42,887
then animals, and check how the body
handles it and what else it hits.

235
00:15:43,217 --> 00:15:45,909
The authors had already
listed these steps.

236
00:15:45,970 --> 00:15:51,964
Two: check the AlphaFold model against a
lab-measured structure, once one exists.

237
00:15:52,275 --> 00:15:57,448
And feed every result, including the
failures, back into the next round.

238
00:15:57,503 --> 00:15:59,194
Three: race them.

239
00:15:59,464 --> 00:16:03,537
An AI route and a classic route,
same target,

240
00:16:03,627 --> 00:16:06,659
same budget, same definition of a hit.

241
00:16:07,089 --> 00:16:10,821
Then we'd know exactly how
much faster AI really is.

242
00:16:11,094 --> 00:16:13,348
So, back to where we started.

243
00:16:13,937 --> 00:16:17,678
How can computer-aided
methods help scientists find

244
00:16:17,718 --> 00:16:20,029
and assess cancer drug candidates?

245
00:16:20,619 --> 00:16:22,930
In this case, at four points.

246
00:16:23,100 --> 00:16:25,050
Picking a target from data.

247
00:16:25,340 --> 00:16:27,651
Predicting the lock's shape.

248
00:16:28,001 --> 00:16:29,622
Designing new keys.

249
00:16:29,662 --> 00:16:32,092
And ranking which ones to make.

250
00:16:32,182 --> 00:16:36,794
That's our first objective,
met for this case.

251
00:16:36,903 --> 00:16:42,558
And our second? These methods helped
the team assess candidates fast.

252
00:16:43,189 --> 00:16:47,823
Screening by the millions
became thirteen molecules made.

253
00:16:48,414 --> 00:16:50,947
And on a road measured in years,

254
00:16:51,137 --> 00:16:55,631
the first hit came in 30 days,
and round two made the key turn.

255
00:16:55,886 --> 00:16:59,399
This works in cells, not yet in people.

256
00:16:59,969 --> 00:17:02,161
The computer narrows the search.

257
00:17:03,152 --> 00:17:05,023
Experiments still decide.

258
00:17:05,290 --> 00:17:09,835
So next time you see a headline
like 'AI discovers a cancer drug'...

259
00:17:10,086 --> 00:17:13,001
...ask where it really is on the road:

260
00:17:13,613 --> 00:17:17,129
computer, protein, cells,

261
00:17:17,801 --> 00:17:20,215
animals, or patients?

262
00:17:20,373 --> 00:17:22,485
AI didn't replace the scientists.

263
00:17:22,816 --> 00:17:27,530
It gave them trial and error at
light speed. Thanks for watching.
